From aabbpp@inet.uni-c.dk Fri Aug  2 04:58:36 1996
Message-ID: <199608020858.KAA00472@inet.uni-c.dk>
Date: Fri, 2 Aug 1996 10:58:04 +0200
From: aabbpp@inet.uni-c.dk <aabbpp@inet.uni-c.dk>
Subject: Re: 21164 cache tags
In-Reply-To: <96Aug1.192807+0100_eet_dst.63439-18934+15@mea.utu.fi>

On Thu, 1 Aug 1996, Matti E Aarnio <mea@mea.cc.utu.fi> wrote:
>> Can someone tell me if the 21164 uses virtual or
>> physical tags in its data caches?  I'm trying to 
>> design some tests to address Linus' concerns but
>> realized I don't know how the cache is tagged.
>
>  Quote from "Alpha Implementations and Architecture":
>
>The instruction cache (Icache) is an 8KB direct mapped, virtual cache.
>
>The data cache (Dcache) is a dual ported, 8KB write thru, read allocate,
>direct mapped, virtually indexed, physically tagged cache with 32-byte
>blocks.  The access time is 2 cycles and the bandwidth is 16 bytes per
>cycle.
>
>The on-chip second-level cache (Scache) is a 96KB, 3-way set associative,
>physical, write back, write allocate, combined data and instruction 
>cache.  It is fully pipelined and supports both 32-byte and 64-byte blocks.
>Access time is 8-cycles and bandwidth is 16 bytes per cycle.

The physical address lines (addr_h[39..5]) are partitioned
into an index field (physical cache address-lines (index_h[25..4]) and a tag 
field (tag_data_h[38..20]). The value on index_h[25..4] is identical to the
value on addr_h[25..4].
The tag size required is Bcache_Size/Block_Size.
On the EB164 board only the index_h[22..4] and tag_data_h[29..20] are used,
which means that a maximum of 2**23 = 8MB cache can be used.
The EB164 also include three jumpers J15, J16 and J17, where the index_h[22..20]
can be broken. This gives you the oppertunerty to decrease the cache size from 
8MB to 1MB (if 8MB cache modules are present). 

	Anders Petersen  

