Message-ID: <199608151522.LAA00554@linux03.amt.tay1.dec.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Aug 1996 11:22:11 -0400
From: jestabro@amt.tay1.dec.com <jestabro@amt.tay1.dec.com>
Subject: Re: Setting time doesn't stick
In-Reply-To: <321256BA.3913@ns.net>

>>> Tim Barthel said:
> Erik Troan wrote:
> > 
> > On Tue, 13 Aug 1996, Tim Barthel wrote:
> > 
> > > I just received a UDB. After I finally tracked down the way to set the
> > > timezone, I set about setting the time using the control panel.
> > > Unfortunately, my time setting is lost when I reboot the machine. I
> > > have RH 3.03. Thanks.
> > 
> > After setting the time, try runnig "/sbin/clock -Aw"
> > 
> 
> Well, thanks for the idea but this command didn't seem to help the 
> situation. Do you have any other ideas? Thanks.

The file /etc/sysconfig/clock, for a system run from ARC console, needs to
contain the line:

CLOCKMODE="ARC"

This is used by the rc.d/rc.sysinit script for parameterizing the "clock"
call when reading from the hardware and setting the kernel time.

To ensure that a changed time is stored to the hardware in the appropriate
format, "clock -A -w" will write the kernel time to the hardware in ARC
console format. Or, use the ARC console facilities to change the hardware
time.

